  {
   "id": "standoff",
   "title": "Three hours of waiting",
   "time": "Dawn to mid-morning",
   "narrative": "Nothing happened for about three hours after sunrise. Contemporary doctrine held that the side that marched against a firm foe lost, and the French expected reinforcements from Brabant, Anjou and Brittany. They were also astride Henry's road. The English, short of food and worn by illness, could not wait. Henry had to provoke the French into action, and that meant giving up his prepared ground.",
   "notes": [
    {
     "at": [
      2.13952,
      50.464649
     ],
     "text": "No move for three hours",
     "kind": "label"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "advance",
   "title": "The English pull up stakes and advance",
   "time": "Mid-morning",
   "narrative": "Henry ordered the whole line forward. The archers tore up their stakes, carried them across the mud and drove them in again about 270 metres from the French, at the limit of a longbow's reach. The French could have struck while the stakes were out of the ground, but did not move. Erpingham is said to have given the signal, and the archers opened a high-angle volley.",
